  "sample": [
    {
      "text": "Getting around the region with limited mobility\n\nAn honest assessment rather than a promotional one. Some of these places are\ndifficult and it is better to know in advance.",
      "source": "guide_accessibility.md",
      "index": 0,
      "produced_by": "chunker.py::split_documents"
    },
    {
      "text": "Corry Vale — Where to stay\n\nPerhaps thirty beds in the entire valley, spread across two pubs and a handful of farmhouse rooms. In summer these are booked months ahead. Camping is permitted on two marked fields and nowhere else.",
      "source": "guide_corry_vale.md",
      "index": 5,
      "produced_by": "chunker.py::split_documents"
    },
    {
      "text": "Givens Mill — Getting around\n\nEverything is on one street along the river. The mill is at one end and the church at the other, eight minutes apart. The riverside path continues in both directions for as far as you want to walk.",
      "source": "guide_givens_mill.md",
      "index": 2,
      "produced_by": "chunker.py::split_documents"
    },
    {
      "text": "Kestrelford — What to see\n\nThe market square on a Saturday morning is the main event and has run continuously since the 1400s. The parish church has a 13th-century tower you can climb for £2. The old trackbed walk runs six miles to the next village along an easy gradient and is the best half-day here.",
      "source": "guide_kestrelford.md",
      "index": 4,
      "produced_by": "chunker.py::split_documents"
    },
    {
      "text": "Pellew Sands — When to go\n\nJune and September for the beach without the crowds. July and August are busy and the town is at its most itself, for better and worse. Winter is bleak, largely closed, and has a following among people who like that sort of thing.",
      "source": "guide_pellew_sands.md",
      "index": 6,
      "produced_by": "chunker.py::split_documents"
    }
  ],
